= Consensus mechanism =

== Overview ==
'''Consensus mechanism''' is a protocol or rule set by which participants in a distributed system establish agreement on a valid shared state or ordered history despite failures, delay, or adversarial behavior within stated assumptions.

Blockchain consensus mechanisms determine which transactions or blocks are accepted and how conflicting histories are resolved. Proof-of-work, proof-of-stake, Byzantine-fault-tolerant protocols, and permissioned schemes use different resources and trust assumptions.

Consensus is not simply majority voting. Some protocols use quorums, weighted stake, computational work, rotating proposers, certificates, or multiple phases of communication.

No consensus mechanism provides unconditional agreement. Safety and liveness guarantees depend on the fault threshold, synchrony assumptions, participant set, cryptography, incentives, and implementation.

== Boundaries ==
* consensus mechanism ≠ majority vote
* consensus ≠ unanimity
* consensus ≠ unconditional finality

